233 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 54 0 R
/E (Taylor, a retired healthcare and research employee, is now struggling to access the care she needs. At age 40, she developed carpal tunnel syndrome, which ultimately led to early retirement and disability. Today, she is carrying approximately $10,000 in medical debt, stemming from treatment for carpal tunnel syndrome and a colonoscopy procedure. Due to the heavy burden of this debt, she frequently avoids recommended care for fear of further costs. Her struggles have been amplified by the impact the debt has had on her credit score, which has created barriers to acquiring a vehicle or even housing. If Taylor had access to cheap preventative care along with affordable treatment through a public hospital system, she would not have been punished for decades of dedicated labor. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
234 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 54 0 R
/E (After a hit-and-run accident and a cascade of devastating losses, including housing and custody of her seven-year-old son; Jennifer's health began to fail. The chronic stress worsened her hyperthyroidism, and despite having insurance, she could not afford the medications her doctor prescribed. Her condition was deteriorating because the out-of-pocket costs were simply out of reach. According to a recent report from the University of Southern California, copays actually cost more than the retail price of the drug almost 25% of the time. When medication exists but remains unaffordable, coverage means nothing. Pharmaceutical companies should be transparent about pricing and make life-sustaining medications accessible to the patients who need them most.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
235 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
